# Plan: poly-A tails survive behind a poly-G tail (`--poly_a`)

**Issue:** [#504](https://github.com/FelixKrueger/TrimGalore/issues/504) — "Less effective PolyA
trimming with v2.3.0", reported by @marenmsk (3'seq libraries, v0.6.10 → v2.3.0).
**Date:** 2026-10-20
**Branch to cut:** `fix/polya-behind-polyg` off `dev`

## Goal

`--poly_a` must remove poly-A tails that sit behind a 3' poly-G run, and tolerate a few stray
non-A bases at the 3' end. Today it leaves both classes untouched, so 2-colour 3'seq libraries
come out of v2.3.0 with full poly-A tails that v0.6.10 removed.

Non-goal: byte-identity with Perl `--polyA`. See "Decision: keep the Cutadapt `--poly-a`
semantics" below — reverting to the v0.6.x algorithm would re-introduce a worse failure.

## Context

### What changed between v0.6.x and v2

Perl `--polyA` was a Cutadapt invocation with `-a "A{20}"` (`0.6.10:trim_galore:1130-1156`;
`-g "T{150}"` for R2). A 3' adapter match removes the match **and everything downstream of it**,
so `[insert][polyA][anything]` lost the tail and the trailing bases together.

v2's `--poly_a` is a different algorithm: `quality::homopolymer_trim_index` walks inward from the
3' end (+1 per A, −2 per non-A, 20% cumulative error gate) and only records a cut where the running
score is positive. Non-A bases **at the very 3' end** drive the score negative before the A-run is
reached, and the cut is never recorded.

`src/trimmer.rs::trim_read` then runs the stages in this order:

| Step | Stage |
|---|---|
| 2.7 | poly-A / poly-T |
| 2.8 | poly-G / poly-C |

Poly-A runs **first**. On a 2-colour read `[insert][polyA][polyG]` the poly-A scan hits the G-run,
gives up, and the poly-G stage then strips the G-run — leaving the poly-A tail exposed in the
output. That is exactly the reporter's symptom ("adapter and polyg are successfully removed, but
the polya is only partially trimmed").

### Reproduction (verified, 2026-10-20, `d471269`)

`target/release/trim_galore --quality 20 --phred33 --polyA --poly_g -a AGATCGGAAGAGC` on synthetic
reads, against `alignment::find_3prime_adapter(seq, "A"×20, 0.1, 1)` as a v0.6.x stand-in (that DP
is the Cutadapt-parity path the `validation` job pins):

| Read | Input | v2.3.0 emits | v0.6.x equivalent |
|---|---|---|---|
| `insert60 + A40` | 100 | 60 | 58 |
| `insert60 + A40 + G15` | 115 | **100** (tail kept) | 58 |
| `insert40 + A60 + G20` | 140 | **100** (tail kept) | 58 |
| `insert60 + A20 + 10 bp junk` | 90 | **88** (tail kept) | 58 |

`--2colour 20` in place of `--quality 20` works around it (G scored as quality 0 → removed in the
quality step, before poly-A looks at the read): the two poly-G reads then come out at exactly 60
and 40. This is the workaround given to the reporter, and it is independent evidence that the
scan itself is sound once the G-run is gone.

### Why it was never caught

- The `validation` job has **no `--polyA` step** (it covers SE, PE, `-a2`, hardtrim5, clock, demux,
  clump_only, uBAM). No Perl baseline was ever taken for this flag.
- No test under `tests/` exercises `--poly_a` at all.
- `--poly_a` shipped as a *new* capability (CHANGELOG line 964, "Generic poly-A trimming for
  mRNA-seq libraries"), so nobody looked for a v0.6.x predecessor — and
  `docs/reference/migration.md:28` still records `| Poly-A trimming | None | Built-in --poly_a |`,
  which is wrong.
- Issue #245 item B (the `-a 'A{15}'` homopolymer DP divergence) was deferred partly on the
  reasoning that *"poly-A trimming has its own dedicated `--poly_a` flag that doesn't go through
  this path"*. That separation is the defect, not a mitigation.

### Decision: keep the Cutadapt `--poly-a` semantics

v2's scan matches modern Cutadapt's `--poly-a` (same +1/−2 scoring and 20% gate). It should stay.
Switching the stage back to an `A{20}` adapter match would re-introduce v0.6.x's over-trimming: on
`test_files/illumina10K_with_polyA.fastq.gz`, the `A{20}` match cut **117 bp** off one read because
of an internal 16-A stretch, where the current scan correctly leaves it alone. Over a 10,898-read
sweep of that fixture the `A{20}` path trimmed more than the current scan on 3,009 reads, 1,965 of
them where the current scan trims nothing. Most are harmless 1-2 bp terminal A's (Perl ran
`-O 1`), but the long-cut class is a real data-loss risk on bisulfite libraries.

So: fix the ordering and the 3'-end tolerance, keep the algorithm.

## Behavior

### B1 — stage order

`trim_read` runs poly-G **before** poly-A. All other stages keep their position: quality (1),
adapter (2), RRBS (2.5), N-trim (3), fixed clipping (4).

Rationale, stated once in the source as one line: poly-G is a terminal sequencer artifact (the
instrument calls no-signal as high-quality G when it overruns the template), so nothing of value
sits behind it, and a poly-A tail behind it is invisible to a 3'-anchored scan.

Scope of the change:

- **R1 / single-end only.** Poly-A trims the 3' end and poly-G trims the 3' end, so they compete
  for the same end on R1/SE. On R2 poly-T is removed from the 5' end and poly-G from the 3' end —
  disjoint, so R2 output is unchanged by the reorder.
- **No existing output changes.** Step 2.7 is skipped entirely when `config.poly_a` is false, which
  is the default and is the case for every current CI validation step. Swapping a skipped stage
  with poly-G cannot alter any pinned md5.

### B2 — bounded 3' offset for the poly-A scan

`poly_a_trim_index` may step over up to `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET = 10` bases at the 3' end before scanning
for the tail. The first offset that yields a cut wins (smallest offset = most conservative), and
the cut removes everything from the A-run start to the read end, including the stepped-over bases.

Verified against the current behaviour (candidate probed in-tree, 2026-10-20):

| Read | current | candidate | |
|---|---|---|---|
| `insert60 + A40` | 60 | 60 | unchanged |
| `insert60 + A40 + G15` | 115 | **60** | fixed |
| `insert40 + A60 + G20` | 120 | **40** | fixed |
| `insert60 + A20 + G20` | 100 | 100 | still needs B1 |
| `insert60 + A20 + junk10` | 90 | **60** | fixed |
| `insert60 + A20 + junk11` | 91 | **60** | fixed |
| `insert60 + A40 + NNNNN` | 60 | 60 | unchanged |
| no tail | 60 | 60 | unchanged |
| internal 16-A run | 76 | 76 | **no over-trim** |
| 3-base tail | 60 | 60 | unchanged |

Two results worth keeping in view:

1. B2 absorbs *short* G-runs on its own (a long A-run can pay for a few trailing G's out of its
   20% error budget), but `A20 + G20` still fails. **B1 is the fix; B2 is the robustness margin.**
   Neither substitutes for the other.
2. The internal-A case is unchanged, so B2 does not re-open the v0.6.x over-trim.

### B3 — 5' branch is already tolerant

`homopolymer_trim_index(.., revcomp = true)` scans forward from the 5' end and returns a clip
*length*, so leading junk is absorbed by the same walk and removed with the head. Probed:
`junk5 + T40` → 45, `junk11 + T40` → 51, both unchanged by the candidate. The offset loop is
therefore applied to the 3' branch only; B2 makes the two branches behave alike rather than
introducing a new asymmetry.

### Edge cases

| Case | Handling |
|---|---|
| Read shorter than 3 bp | existing `n < 3` guard in `homopolymer_trim_index`; offset loop is bounded by `n` |
| Empty record | existing `!record.is_empty()` guard at the call site |
| Read is all A | unchanged (current returns 0; offset 0 already cuts) |
| Tail < 3 bp | unchanged — still ignored, at every offset |
| `--no_poly_g --poly_a` on 2-colour data | tails behind G-runs longer than B2's margin still survive. Explicit opt-out; documented, not worked around. See Questions Q1. |
| `--poly_a` on R2 | unchanged (B3) |
| `--rrbs` | RRBS is step 2.5, ahead of both poly stages; unaffected |

## Signature

```rust
/// Maximum number of 3' bases the poly-A scan steps over before the tail.
const MAX_TAIL_OFFSET: usize = 10;

/// Find the start index of a poly-A tail (3' end) or poly-T head (5' end).
///
/// At the 3' end the tail may sit up to `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET` bases short of the read
/// end; the stepped-over bases are removed with the tail.
pub fn poly_a_trim_index(sequence: &[u8], revcomp: bool) -> usize
```

`homopolymer_trim_index` keeps its current signature and behaviour — poly-G uses it unchanged,
and offset-0 is correct there because dark-cycle G-runs are strictly terminal.

## Implementation outline

Four commits on `fix/polya-behind-polyg`.

### Commit 1 — reorder the poly stages

1. `src/trimmer.rs`: move the poly-G block (currently "2.8", `src/trimmer.rs:~220-232`) above the
   poly-A block (currently "2.7", `~192-212`). Renumber the two comment headers so poly-G is 2.7
   and poly-A is 2.8.
2. Add one line above the poly-G block giving the invariant (current state, no history — see
   CLAUDE.md "Code comments"). Keep the existing #321 explanation; it is still accurate.
3. Update the `trim_read` doc comment (`src/trimmer.rs:95-101`), whose processing-order list omits
   the poly stages entirely. Add them in their real positions.
4. `src/quality.rs:501`: the comment on `test_poly_g_after_poly_a_removal` asserts the old order in
   prose. The assertion itself is order-independent; reword the comment and rename the test to
   `test_poly_g_tail` so it does not encode a stale pipeline order.

### Commit 2 — bounded 3' offset

1. `src/quality.rs`: add `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET` and rewrite `poly_a_trim_index`'s body as the offset
   loop (3' branch only; the `revcomp` branch delegates to `homopolymer_trim_index` unchanged).
   Replace the "thin wrapper … for backwards compatibility" doc comment.
2. Call site in `src/trimmer.rs` is unchanged — it already calls `poly_a_trim_index`.

### Commit 3 — tests

1. `src/quality.rs` unit tests: tail behind 15 G, tail behind 20 G (expect *not* trimmed at this
   level — B1 covers it), 10 bp junk, 11 bp junk, internal 16-A run unchanged, 3-base tail
   unchanged, `n < 3`, all-A, and the three R2 poly-T cases from B3.
2. New `tests/integration_poly.rs` — the gap that let this ship. Write a synthetic FASTQ with
   `std::fs::write` into a `tempfile::TempDir` (the pattern in
   `tests/integration_partial_output.rs:525`), run the binary with `--poly_a --poly_g -a
   AGATCGGAAGAGC --dont_gzip`, and assert **no output read ends in ≥10 A**. Cover
   `[insert][polyA][polyG]`, `[insert][polyA][junk]`, and a clean `[insert][polyA]` control.
3. Second integration case: same input with `--2colour 20` instead of `--quality 20`, asserting the
   same postcondition — this is the path recommended to the reporter, so it should be guarded.

### Commit 4 — docs and CHANGELOG

1. `docs/src/content/docs/reference/migration.md:28` — `| Poly-A trimming | None | Built-in
   --poly_a |` is factually wrong. v0.6.x had `--polyA` (Cutadapt `-a "A{20}"`). Correct the row.
2. `docs/src/content/docs/reference/migration.md:51-53` — the `-a 'A{N}'` note calls that form "a
   v0.6.x workaround that pre-dates `--poly_a`". It *was* v0.6.x `--polyA`. Say so, and say that
   `--poly_a` is a different algorithm (Cutadapt `--poly-a`) rather than a renamed one, so a
   migrating user should expect different output, not identical output.
3. `docs/src/content/docs/modes/poly.md` — add the stage order (poly-G before poly-A) and the
   2-colour interaction, in the existing register. Keep the "When to use which" table; add a 3'seq
   / poly-A-selected row pointing at `--2colour`.
4. `src/cli.rs:365-368` — the `--poly_a` help text says "Runs after adapter trimming, so poly-A
   tails hidden behind adapters are also removed". Still true; extend to name poly-G.
5. `CHANGELOG.md` — one sentence, repo format, crediting @marenmsk for the report.

## Efficiency

The offset loop is O(K·n) with K ≤ 11 and n = read length, worst case when no tail is found. Reads
are short and `--poly_a` is opt-in and off by default, so this is not on any hot path that matters;
the adapter DP in step 2 dominates by orders of magnitude. Early return on the first offset that
cuts means the common case (tail flush with the 3' end) costs exactly one scan, as today. No
allocation — the loop slices.

## Integration

- **Reads:** `config.poly_a`, `config.poly_g`, `record.seq`.
- **Writes:** `record` (truncate / `clip_5prime`), `TrimResult::poly_a_trimmed`,
  `TrimResult::poly_g_trimmed`.
- **Order:** quality → adapter → RRBS → **poly-G → poly-A** → N-trim → fixed clipping.
- `bp_after_cutadapt` is captured before all poly stages (`src/trimmer.rs:161`), so the
  cutadapt-section report figures and the #232 invariant are untouched.
- `src/parallel.rs` holds no copy of this logic — it calls `trimmer::trim_read` at
  `src/parallel.rs:495-496` and `:1133` and only aggregates stats. One edit site.
- `poly_a_trimmed` counts every base the stage removed, so after B2 it includes the stepped-over
  bases. The report label is "Poly-A/T bases removed", which stays accurate.
- Report line order in `src/report.rs:401-425` prints poly-A before poly-G. Cosmetic; left alone
  so the reporter's existing reports stay comparable.

## Assumptions

1. The reporter's reads are `[insert][polyA][polyG]` — inferred from "adapter and polyg are
   successfully removed, but the polya is only partially trimmed", not confirmed on her data. The
   reply asks for her `Reads with poly-A/T trimmed` count to confirm. **If that count is already
   high, the diagnosis is wrong and this plan does not apply.**
2. Poly-G auto-detection fired on her run (she passed neither `--poly_g` nor `--no_poly_g`), which
   her description implies.
3. Dark-cycle poly-G is strictly terminal, so offset 0 remains correct for the poly-G scan.
4. `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET = 10` is a judgement call, not a derived constant. Fixed, not configurable —
   no new flag. Raising it trades tolerance for over-trim risk on A-rich inserts.
5. `find_3prime_adapter(seq, "A"×20, 0.1, 1)` is a faithful v0.6.x stand-in for the comparison
   numbers above. Caveat: #245 item B records a known DP divergence from Cutadapt on homopolymer
   adapters, so those numbers are indicative of the *class* of v0.6.x behaviour, not exact Perl
   output.

## Validation

| # | What to verify | How | Expected |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | The reported case is fixed end-to-end | Build, run `--quality 20 --phred33 --polyA --poly_g -a AGATCGGAAGAGC` on the synthetic set in Context | `A40+G15` → 60 bp, `A60+G20` → 40 bp, no read ends in ≥10 A |
| 2 | B1 and B2 are independently load-bearing | Run the `A20+G20` case with B2 only, then with both | B2 alone: tail kept. Both: trimmed |
| 3 | No over-trim regression | `poly_a_trim_index` on the internal-16-A read; full run over `test_files/illumina10K_with_polyA.fastq.gz` | internal case unchanged; no read loses >tail length |
| 4 | No pinned output moves | `cargo test` (509 lib tests) + full `validation` job | all green, no md5 step changes |
| 5 | R2 is untouched | `--paired --poly_a` on `polyAT_R1/R2`, before and after | R2 output byte-identical across the change |
| 6 | The documented workaround still works | integration case with `--2colour 20` | same postcondition as case 1 |

Validation 4 is the one that matters most for merge confidence: the claim that no existing output
changes rests on `config.poly_a` being false everywhere in CI, which the full job settles.

## Questions or ambiguities

- **Q1 (Open).** `--no_poly_g --poly_a` on 2-colour data still leaves tails behind long G-runs.
  Recommendation: leave it. `--no_poly_g` is an explicit opt-out and honouring it is correct;
  document the interaction instead of special-casing it.
- **Q2 (Open).** Add a Perl `--polyA` step to the `validation` job? Recommendation: **no.** The
  divergence is intentional (see "Decision"), so byte-identity is the wrong gate and would
  immediately fail. The behavioural postcondition in `tests/integration_poly.rs` is the right
  guard and runs on every `cargo test` without conda or Perl.
- **Q3 (Open).** `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET = 10` — no data behind the exact value. 10 covers every junk
  length observed in the fixtures and leaves the internal-A case untouched. Worth revisiting if a
  real dataset shows longer trailing artifacts.
- **Q4 (Open).** #245 item B remains unresolved and is adjacent: the `-a 'A{N}'` route is what the
  reporter is being told to use as a fallback. Not in scope here; worth re-opening separately now
  that the "the dedicated flag covers it" reasoning has failed.

## Self-Review

**Efficiency.** O(K·n), K ≤ 11, early-return on the common case. No allocation. Off the default
path entirely. Checked that nothing hoists `poly_a_trim_index` into a per-base loop.

**Logic.** Checked that B1 and B2 are independent and neither is redundant — probed `A20+G20`
specifically to confirm B2 alone does not cover it, and `A40+G15` to confirm B2 alone *does*. The
plan originally claimed B2 could not absorb any G-run; the probe disproved that and the Behavior
section now states the real boundary.

**Edge cases.** `n < 3`, empty record, all-A, 3-base tail, tail exactly at `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET` and one
past it, R2/5' branch, `--rrbs` interaction. The offset loop is bounded by `n`, so no slice can
panic on short reads.

**Integration.** Confirmed `trimmer::trim_read` is the only implementation of the stage order
(`grep` for `trim_read(` and for `poly_a|poly_g|homopolymer` across `src/`): `parallel.rs`,
`specialty.rs`, `clump*.rs` hold no copy. Confirmed `bp_after_cutadapt` is captured upstream of both
poly stages, so the #232 report invariant is unaffected. Confirmed the reorder cannot move any
pinned md5, because `config.poly_a` is false in every CI validation step.

**Remaining risks.**

1. Assumption 1 is unconfirmed on the reporter's data. The reply asks for the one number that
   settles it; if it comes back high, re-diagnose before implementing.
2. `MAX_TAIL_OFFSET` is a judgement call (Q3).
3. The v0.6.x comparison figures inherit #245 item B's caveat — indicative of the behaviour class,
   not exact Perl output.
